# PsionNet: a netBook Pro on Windows CE, dialling through a MODEM entry on
# COM1. The Debian version of etc/ppp/peers/psion-ce-modem.
#
# PsionNet starts it as
#
#     pppd call psion-ce-modem /dev/ttyUSB0 logfile ~/.local/state/psionnet/ppp.log
#
# CE drives the port as if a Hayes modem were attached, so the fake modem
# answers like one -- OK to every AT command, CONNECT to the dial -- and then
# PPP starts. It logs every byte to /var/log/psionnet-fakemodem.log. Windows
# CE's "Direct Connection" negotiates PPP and then carries no traffic; dial-up
# is the route. (The macOS file explains both at length.)
connect "/usr/bin/python3 /usr/lib/psionnet/fakemodem.py"

# 19200, NOT 115200: CE's modem entry sets the line speed, and on the netBook
# Pro it defaults to 19200. If you raise it in the device's modem settings,
# raise this to match.
19200
10.0.2.1:10.0.2.2

ms-dns 1.1.1.1
ms-dns 9.9.9.9

noauth
local
nodetach
nodefaultroute
crtscts
asyncmap 0
mtu 1500
mru 1500
lcp-echo-interval 30
lcp-echo-failure 4
ipparam psionnet
debug
